_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:513:Raw types are `*const T` / `*mut T` (slice 24). Dereference/store use unary `*` and place assignment; `as` handles the decided reference/pointer and pointer/integer conversions, all gated as required by [OD98](../docs/decisions.md#od98). Pointer addresses do not carry integer provenance; every raw access requires typed validity/provenance/aliasing premises (Core Q3.7–Q3.11). Reference-derived pointers stay confined to the borrow, except explicit kept foreign retention with the receipt discipline ([OD64](../docs/decisions.md#od64), Core Q3.12). A write derived from shared &T remains forbidden ([OD112](../docs/decisions.md#od112)).
_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:515:Slice 25 declares C bindings as `extern fn`, optionally `trusted extern fn`, explicit `with(...)`, mandatory semicolon and no body. `#[link_name = "symbol"]` and `#[export_name = "symbol"]` payload forms are recommendations (OPEN-15), not an inferred `extern "C" {…}` block. Direct calls only: foreign function values and `extern "C" fn` types are not admitted ([OD4](../docs/decisions.md#od4)). FFI-safe types exclude enums ([OD110](../docs/decisions.md#od110)); C structs cross by value at every size ([OD122](../docs/decisions.md#od122)). `kept` is decided, but marker placement, explicit call-only annotation and paired-ending source signatures are OPEN-23. Semantic validity obligations must not be replaced by inventing an `unsafe {…}` source block.
_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:601:Postfix `?`, value `while … else`, and postfix member `->` are explicitly described as removed in the [frozen grammar](../legacy/grammar/concrete.ebnf) and legacy syntax materials; **no specific deciding row in decisions.md was found for those three removals**. Their historical refusal is evidence, not an invented OD citation. **OPEN-25** recommends carrying the refusals forward and asks the owner to ratify them. Do not confuse postfix `->` with the retained function result arrow. Similarly this sheet does not treat block comments/exponents as an owner-decided removal without OPEN-1/5 approval.
_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:631:23. **OPEN-23 — Raw/FFI qualifiers and retention spelling (24/25).** Recommend `kept p: *mut T`, unmarked pointer parameter call-only by default, ptr-qualified raw APIs with a documented exception for decided read_owned(p), paired Kept receipt ending calls, extern fn without ABI-string blocks. Alternative: explicit `call_only` and `kept` markers on all pointer parameters, global raw operation names. Reason: Q3.12 decides kept/Kept, not every token position or qualification; OD64 confinement and OD13 move-out must remain visible.
_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:633:25. **OPEN-25 — Historical refusals without located deciding rows.** Recommend ratify refusal of postfix ?, value while…else and postfix ->; keep only function result ->. Alternative: reopen each as a separate language proposal. Reason: the frozen grammar documents removal, but no matching authoritative deciding anchor was found; attaching an unrelated OD would invent a decision.
docs/decisions.md:122:These items were undesigned when the chapters were cleaned up on 2026-11-03. **The owner approved their design provisionally on 2026-11-05** ([the decided rows](#decided-2026-11-05-core): allocator identities Q1, allocation contracts Q1–Q2, the heap-array view Q3, static text Q4, capability inference Q5, unsafe contracts Q3.7–Q3.12, static refusal Q6, grants Q7, semantic objects Q8–Q9). Where a chapter still says **undecided: see the target Core proposal**, read the decided row; the rule is rewritten in Lean at stage A2b and frozen at A2c. The proposal is owned by
docs/decisions.md:137:  APIs, including the resource backing process services, undecided on 2026-11-03, are provisionally decided by rows Q7.1–Q7.11 ([rows](#decided-2026-11-05-core); process spawning is deferred).
docs/decisions.md:142:  and foreign-mutation taxonomy and call-only annotation syntax, undecided on 2026-11-03, are provisionally decided by rows Q3.7–Q3.12 and Q9.7 ([rows](#decided-2026-11-05-core)); OD64's confinement
docs/decisions.md:173:The owner answered every row of the 2026-11-05 decision page, in the terminal. The rows that revise a recorded rule were answered one by one: Q5.1/Q5.4, Q6.4, Q2.8, Q9.15 with P3, Q7.11 and Q4.7. Q3.12, Q9.19, Q7.10, Q1.6, P2, P4, P9, P13, P16 and P17a/b were also answered directly. The remaining Core rows were accepted as recommended, and the remaining project rows were approved on the owner's condition that Claude and Codex agree and that each is the best long-term design (both confirmed; every row carries Codex agreement). The full rows (question, options, reason, recommendation, agreement, revision and notes) are in [core_rows.json](archive/decisions-2026-11-05/core_rows.json) and [other_rows.json](archive/decisions-2026-11-05/other_rows.json); the concrete rules behind them are in [R4_RATIONALE.md](archive/decisions-2026-11-05/R4_RATIONALE.md), and where R4 and a row differ, the row governs.
docs/decisions.md:197:- **Q1.6** Who owns a child allocator’s backing? — Child owns backing; parent brand exposed in type. *Revises or adds: Clarify/extend allocator interface.*
docs/decisions.md:207:- **Q2.3** In what order are contents destroyed? — Index order 0..len−1, then the backing; OrderedMap in ascending key order, key then value, then the nodes.
docs/decisions.md:224:- **Q3.12** How are extern pointer parameters marked, and how does C keep memory past the call? — (a) `kept` parameter marker; `Kept<H>` receipt ended only by the paired extern. *Revises or adds: New design + spelling: owner decision.*
docs/decisions.md:252:- **Q7.10** Do you approve the linux-x86_64-hosted grant table? — Approve R4 F6's complete grant table below as part of this row: every launch configuration, operation signature, permission, narrowing rule and ending contract. Standard-stream operations require Console (Q7.11), not Device; where the R4 F6 table says with(Device) for StdIn/StdOut/StdErr, read with(Console). A missing configured grant refuses startup with exit 125; a trap exits 134. *Revises or adds: New target contract: owner decision.* *Extended 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): the stack target contract (adequate stack excludes exhaustion, launch refusal 125 when the reservation is smaller than the bound; then a defined outcome for inadequate stack) is a future target-contract obligation, not a current guarantee.*
docs/decisions.md:299:- **P17a** Default arguments — Default arguments: a Permanent refusal, reopened only by a new decision from you. They conflict with visible arguments.
docs/decisions.md:663:    (extended to functions), Q7.10 and Q8.5 (extended by the stack target-contract obligations of Q14, which are future target work, not current guarantees). Unchanged: STMT-1, STMT-2, OD101 (general trap semantics), Q6.3–Q6.6
- **Q9.15** Where do normative definitions and documentation live? — One main normative Lean file with doc comments. *Revises or adds: Generated-chapter requirement (philosophy rule 6 / earlier roadmap commitment): replaced by one normative Lean file with doc comments and examples, a browsable rendering of that Lean, and coverage-gated retirement of prose chapters; your decision of 2026-11-04.*
- **Q9.16** What Lean dependencies may the specification use? — Core Lean; supporting Batteries; owner exceptions for Mathlib.
- **Q9.17** May spec proofs use `native_decide`? — Kernel-only authoritative spec/A2 proofs.
- **Q9.18** How is the old compiler retained? — Frozen isolated `legacy/`; no normative fallback.
- **Q9.19** How do approval, formalization and Core freeze relate? — A2a → A2b → A2c; Spec.lean headed PROPOSED until freeze; R-0560 depends on the freeze. *Revises or adds: Process: owner decision.* Revised 2026-11-06 by the [long-term changes](#decided-project-long-term-2026-11-06): integration (A2i) comes between A2b and the A2c freeze, and the modular layout (R-0560) precedes the freeze.

<a id="decided-2026-11-05-project"></a>**Project rows:**

- **P1** Where agent discretion ends — Claude and Codex reason every question from the philosophy and recommend; they never pick a language answer through code, fixtures, diagnostics or docs. 'Important' = anything that revises a decided rule, changes observable language meaning you have not ruled on, or that you reserved. Every important decision comes to you.
- **P2** Priority order when principles conflict — Soundness and provability > transparency (with auditability, honest trust boundaries and compiler-owned identity) > one authoritative definition (checked derived views allowed) > explicitness at the use site > brevity. The philosophy guides open choices and never silently reopens decided rules.
- **P3** Lean is the spec: confirm the amendment — Lean is the specification: confirmed together with Q9.15 (revision below).
- **P4** Duplicate enum variants or methods (bug 313) — Refuse duplicate variant names in one enum and duplicate method names in one impl, with a new rule and diagnostic, the same as duplicate fields.
- **P5** legacy/ is a byte-frozen snapshot, never patched or run — legacy/ is main's tree at the move commit, byte-frozen. A root check compares its tree hash, allowing only deletions a satisfied RETENTION.tsv entry authorizes. No legacy gate ever runs. The oracle is the tag-built artifact (P13), not the legacy/ copy.
- **P6** Retention manifest gates deleting legacy/ — RETENTION.tsv at the root gives every legacy asset (corpus, regressions, examples, std, bug ledger, site, docs, research) a destination and deletion criterion. Revised 2026-11-06 ([restart row](#decided-project-restart)): nothing is deleted at B3; legacy/, the old pipeline included, is deleted way later (LD: once the new compiler implements a full real program such as RocksDB, ethrex or similar), when RETENTION.tsv has no live entry, a final ideas sweep has dispositioned every idea and the owner explicitly approves.
- **P7** Prove the new landing path before moving — T0: land beside the old tree the new root CI, required-checks.txt (enforced by train watch and land), RETENTION.tsv, the task disposition table, ORACLE.lock with the oracle artifact, and ISSUES.tsv. T1: rehearse the move on a throwaway branch: fresh clone, build, hook, exact-SHA CI, train --dry-run land; a broken build, a failing gate and a missing required check must each be refused. T2: the move in one train.
- **P8** Server services under scripts/ — Keep only landing services: train, review, hook, landing bot. The status page, work queue, fuzz farm, perf tracking and nightly campaign all stop and are recorded as archived in RETENTION.tsv; each returns only with a roadmap task.
- **P9** Website during the transition — The website goes down at the move and is redone from scratch with the new compiler; no frozen playground is served. Unpublishing the live site is an outward action whose moment the owner confirms.
- **P10** What 'closed' means for old-compiler bugs — Old-compiler defects become 'retired with the old implementation', never 'fixed'. Each keeps its reproducer, ruling and structural class; 106 reproducers feed the oracle comparisons. Bugs the frozen browser compiler still shows stay visible while the site serves it.
- **P11** Std bugs and rulings are carried — Add a 'std' class: 12 std bugs move with std as contracts plus regressions. The 112 language rulings (26 still open) keep their ruling and become conformance examples.
- **P12** Issue record from day one — From day one, ISSUES.tsv at the root: id, date, area (spec|proof|compiler|launcher|ci|migration), evidence, owner, severity, closure condition, status, with a small root check. Spec, proof, launcher, CI and migration defects go there. The full bug ledger and tool return with slice 1.
- **P13** concrete-ci-2 as the only CI host — CI runs on concrete-ci-2 (self-hosted) and on GitHub-hosted runners as a regular second pool, not only a fallback. Jobs that need ci-2 caches run there; the oracle is fetched as a release asset verified by digest; a check neither pool can run is incomplete and blocks landing. Self-hosted runners never run pull_request jobs.
- **P14** ci-3 handover: an exception to the archive requirement — Done by the owner on 2026-11-04: concrete-ci-3 was handed to stabileo; its fuzz corpus, perf history and leanprof data were not copied (an exception to the archive requirement).
- **P15** New roadmap: retiring obligations — Accept the new single-file ROADMAP.md (wip/new-roadmap, reconciled: prepare → rehearse → move, assurance wording per Q8.1, Phase E per P18, decides nothing itself): 79 tasks kept as rows, 345 folded into area tasks with their obligations, 109 deferred with triggers, 7 retired, 15 done.
- **P16** Status changes in the refusal list — Accept the new statuses in PHILOSOPHY.md. Now Permanent: operator overloading (OD26), capability hiding (authority rule 5), derived equality, mutable global state (OD8), umbrella capabilities and aliases, old/quantifiers in contracts (OD117), lexical shadowing (OD67), and ghost code that affects execution. Ghost code itself is admitted as decided (OD81). Foreign function values reopen when typed extern fn types are admitted (OD4); concurrency reopens on R-0399's trigger.
- **P17a** Default arguments — Default arguments: a Permanent refusal, reopened only by a new decision from you. They conflict with visible arguments.
- **P17b** Compile-time evaluation and broad #[cfg] — comptime and broad #[cfg]: keep evidence-based reopening, only with a workload, a proof story, phase separation and an audit report, and only by a new decision from you.
- **P18** Phase E: contracts and termination within OD115–OD117 — Phase E: no source old, quantifier, implication or snapshot construct (OD117); entry/exit and frame properties are Lean theorems (a source frame clause would be a new decision for you); spec functions have a body and are total (OD115); ordinary functions have no total category (OD116) and get per-function termination theorems where wanted. R-0524 becomes 'Frame and entry/exit theorems in Lean, no old'; R-0526 stays 'checked totality of spec functions'.
- **P19** Mutation testing for the new compiler — Retire the old mutation-harness defect work (R-0518). The new compiler gets its own mutation harness under R-0498, not a port of the old one.
- **P20** One home for binding decisions — Move the still-binding roadmap decisions (new compiler, retention contract, authority, allocation, Lean as the spec) into docs/decisions.md beside your language answers. The dated log becomes history; ROADMAP.md links to decisions.md and restates nothing.
- **P21** Retention records outlive legacy/ — RETENTION.tsv, ISSUES.tsv, required-checks.txt, scripts/oracle/ and docs/archive/roadmap-2026-11/ (with task_disposition.tsv) are part of the day-one root, outside legacy/, so they survive deleting it. *Revised 2026-11-06 (owner, verbatim: "do the whole movement"): of docs/archive/roadmap-2026-11/, the files that are decision sources or disposition records stay at the root (task_disposition.tsv, the decision log ROADMAP_DECISIONS.md, the task register ROADMAP_TASKS.md, ROADMAP_RECONCILIATION.md, ROADMAP_RESTRUCTURE.md with its table, and the archive README); the old control plane ROADMAP.md, ROADMAP_BACKLOG.md, ROADMAP_DIRECTION.md and WORK_QUEUE.md were duplicates of their frozen legacy/ copies apart from the move's mechanical rewrites, and nothing cites them as a decision source, so their root copies were removed and links point at the legacy/ copies, which stay until the legacy deletion point. The other docs/archive/ records (the site takedown record, the old bug ledger's disposition, the landing audit and the dated decision archives) stay at the root.*
- **P22** The work-queue dispatcher — Stop the dispatcher for the lean start. Agents are started by hand from the roadmap; bring the dispatcher back only when there is enough parallel work to need it.
- **P23** Precedence between decisions, rows and drafts — Recorded decisions govern. An accepted row supersedes a recorded rule only through an explicitly identified, owner-approved revision (Q5.1/Q5.4 for OD45/OD87, Q6.4 for TYPE-22, Q2.8 for TRAIT-1, Q9.15/P3 for generated chapters). R4's replacements govern conflicting draft recommendations. Before your acceptance is recorded, every passage R4 F18 lists is reconciled (capability inference, generated chapters, roadmap authority, assurance wording, restart order and retention, Phase E, freeze dependencies, superseded Core recommendations), and historical conflicting text is marked superseded.

<a id="decided-project"></a>
## Project and architecture decisions

These are the binding project and architecture decisions, moved here from the archived roadmap
decision log (decision row P20, 2026-11-05) so that language and project decisions have one home.
Each entry summarizes the decision and links its verbatim record in the
[2026-11 archive](archive/roadmap-2026-11/ROADMAP_DECISIONS.md), which stays as history; where an
entry and its archived record differ, the entry and its revision notes govern. The roadmap,

codex
1. OPEN-23 recommends `kept p: *mut T`, but kept parameters consume owned allocations. Section 6 also incorrectly permits borrow-derived pointers to escape through kept retention; they can never reach kept parameters. See the approved [Q3.12 row](docs/archive/decisions-2026-11-05/core_rows.json).

2. Kept-marker placement and the call-only default are already decided: `kept buf: H`, with no marker meaning call-only. OPEN-23 incorrectly reopens both choices. See [Q3.12’s options and approved recommendation](docs/archive/decisions-2026-11-05/core_rows.json).

3. Section 6 labels exhaustive resource signatures OPEN-4, but Q7.10 expressly approves the complete hosted grant table, including operation signatures, permissions and ending contracts. Transcribing that table remains work; approving it again does not. See [Q7.10](docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-05-core).

4. OPEN-25 incorrectly treats postfix `?` refusal as undecided. The owner approved the refusal statuses, and the binding list explicitly makes postfix `?` permanent. Only the other two historical removals need the proposed ratification. See [the refusal decision](docs/decisions.md#decided-project-refusals) and [the explicit refusal](docs/PHILOSOPHY.md#what-concrete-refuses).

5. Section 8 misses a FIRST/FOLLOW conflict: optional arm commas put `-` in FOLLOW(expr), while `sum` consumes it as subtraction. In `0 => x -1 => y`, one token cannot distinguish subtraction from the next negative pattern. Require arm separators. See [arm and expression productions]( _review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md#patterns).

6. The refusal inventory omits decided surface exclusions including default arguments, source-generating macros/derive, and broad `#[cfg]` or general compile-time evaluation without reopening approval. Add their statuses and diagnostic directions. See [approved refusal decisions](docs/decisions.md#decided-project-refusals) and [the complete refusal list](docs/PHILOSOPHY.md#what-concrete-refuses).

