- <a id="decided-project-predictable-policy"></a>**The predictable policy** (owner, 2026-11-06: "yes
  this is critical"). An optional policy a module or project turns on, built on the per-function facts
  ([facts report](#decided-project-facts-report)): every function in its scope allocates nothing,
  never blocks and calls no foreign code, and, where the policy demands it, has termination evidence
  and a stack bound. Where the policy is on, a violation is refused at compile time, naming the
  function, the fact and its source; the facts query reports which policy covers each function. It
  follows the slice-0 facts report and query and is required from slice 1 (R-0576), not deferred.
  Admission requires established evidence of no allocation, no blocking and no foreign calls; an
  unknown or incomplete fact is refused. The policy explicitly states whether named trusted
  assumptions are acceptable, as required by Q13. It takes up the idea of the
  legacy predictable profile and of R-0220 (PredictableV1, deferred with a trigger), replacing that
  trigger by this decision; unlike R-0220's draft it admits no allocation at all, bounded or not.
  It is a policy over reported facts, not a function category (OD116) and not a build profile that
  changes behaviour (OD124).
- <a id="decided-project-reproducible-builds"></a>**Reproducible builds from slice 0** (owner,
  2026-11-06: "yes to this too"; revised 2026-11-07: "can we have reproducible builds since the
  beginning right?" and "ok add the reproducible asap"). From slice 0, the same source, compiler
  revision, toolchain pin and target give byte-identical output: the BIR, LLVM IR text, LCB job and
  output, and final binary (R-0577). The toolchain pin covers the nix flake, LCB revision and
  LLVM/clang version. Artifacts contain no timestamps, absolute paths or host names
  (`-ffile-prefix-map`, `SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH`, deterministic link order), and compiler output never
  depends on hash-map iteration order. CI builds every slice-0 example twice in different
  directories and on two machines (a GitHub runner and concrete-ci-2), compares SHA-256 and fails on
  any difference. `concrete facts` carries the build identity: compiler SHA, toolchain pins and
  target. This moves the reproducibility half of R-0104 (merged into R-0149 in phase G) to B0;
  R-0149 keeps incremental-build equivalence. The slice-0 worker owns the work on `wip/slice0` when
  Claude capacity returns; work started on `wip/slice0-repro`.
- <a id="decided-project-concrete-toml"></a>**Policies and declared assumptions live in
  `Concrete.toml`** (owner, 2026-11-06: "concrete.toml is fine", in place of a separate policy
  file). A project's policies, such as the
  [predictable policy](#decided-project-predictable-policy), and its declared assumptions (target,
  heap, stack, foreign functions and the trusted surface) are written in the project's
  `Concrete.toml`. There is no separate policy or assumptions file.
  - *Typed, versioned data.* Policy and assumption sections are typed and versioned; an unknown
    section, key or version is refused, never ignored.
  - *Only tightening.* A module may tighten a project policy, never relax it.
  - *Dependencies.* A dependency's `Concrete.toml` never relaxes the consumer's policy: dependencies
    are checked against the consumer's policy, transitively and fail-closed.
  - *Assumptions.* Assumptions are declared only where the facts cannot derive them. The facts query
1237:| OD122 | <a id="od122"></a>A `repr(C)` struct returned from an extern function (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the platform and stdlib documents). Question: Whether a `#[repr(C)]` struct returned from an `extern fn` is returned as the platform's C calling convention returns it, at every size. Bears on FFI-2, FFI-11. The current compiler: a returned struct of at most 8 bytes comes back as one 64-bit integer, stored as 8 bytes into the struct's slot even when the struct is smaller (a 4-byte `in_addr` returned by `inet_makeaddr` reads back wrong natively); a larger one is returned as an LLVM aggregate, which matches C on x86-64 and AArch64 only for some layouts. [ABI.md](../legacy/docs/platform/ABI.md) promised returns of at most 8 bytes and listed larger ones as not promised. Options: (a) every size, as C, classified by the `Layout` authority (R-0502); (b) at most 8 bytes (or 16), a larger return refused in an `extern fn` signature; (c) none, a returned struct going through an out-pointer the binding declares. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/platform and docs/stdlib, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-122 | see OPEN-122 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Every size per C ABI via `Layout` |
1239:| OD124 | <a id="od124"></a>Profiles in the specification (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the platform and stdlib documents). Question: Whether the profiles (the admission profiles Safe, Predictable and Proof, and the build profiles) belong to the language specification, in a chapter of their own, and whether a profile may change what an accepted program does. Bears on ARITH-9, OPEN-97, PASS-3, PASS-4. The current compiler: a profile only refuses or reports: `--check predictable` reports its five gates (no recursion, bounded loops, no allocation, no extern calls, no `File`, `Network` or `Process`), `[policy] predictable = true` in `Concrete.toml` turns a violation into E0610, and no profile changes the behaviour of a program it accepts; ARITH-9 states that for arithmetic. [PROFILES.md](../legacy/docs/platform/PROFILES.md) says a profile never changes what a program means, while OPEN-97 asks whether a build profile may omit a run-time check. Options: (a) no profiles chapter: profiles are tooling, and the spec states only that no profile changes an accepted program's behaviour; (b) a profiles chapter whose entries state each profile's admission rules as refusals, with that same guarantee; (c) as (b), with build profiles allowed to differ as OPEN-97 decides. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/platform and docs/stdlib, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-124 | see OPEN-124 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Profiles chapter of refusals; no behaviour change |
1251:| OD136 | <a id="od136"></a>std's `process_exit` (added 2026-10-30, raised while reviewing the absorption of docs/compiler and docs/platform). Question: Whether std keeps `process_exit`, which ends the process through libc `exit` without running any deferred call or consuming the caller's owned values. Bears on STMT-7, OWN-2 and TRUST-4. The current compiler: `std.process.process_exit(code: i32) with(Process)` exists and calls libc `exit`; the language itself has no exit operation (STMT-7). The design record [MAIN_EXIT_MODEL.md](../legacy/docs/platform/MAIN_EXIT_MODEL.md) calls an exit operation deliberately absent, since in a linear language it would bypass the proof that every owned value is consumed. Options: (a) keep it, stating that it skips cleanup and that `Process` is what grants it; (b) remove it, status flowing back through `main`'s return only. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the review of the absorption of docs/platform, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-136 | see OPEN-136 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Remove |
1255:| OD140 | <a id="od140"></a>Implicitly dropped results of `free` and of a deferred call (added 2026-10-30, raised by the spec quality audit of 2026-10-30). Question: Whether the result of `free(h)` (the pointee, of type `T`) when `T` is Copy, and a deferred call's Copy result, may be dropped without `discard`, as exceptions to OWN-7@3 (OD29). Bears on OWN-7, OWN-17, MEM-5 and OWN-15. The current compiler: both accepted: `free(h);` on a `Heap<Int>` (whose result is the `Int` moved out of the cell: `let v: Int = free(h);` after `alloc(42)` gives 42 on both consumers) and `defer f();` with `fn f() -> u8` compile and run, while OWN-7@3 (decided OD29) drops a non-Unit result only through `discard(e)`. OWN-17 states the current compiler's drop of a deferred call's result, and MEM-5 and OWN-15 describe `free(h);` as a statement; neither is named as an exception in OWN-7. Options: (a) both are exceptions, stated in OWN-7; (b) neither is, so each needs `discard`; (c) `free` is an exception and a deferred call's result is not. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the spec quality audit, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-140 | see OPEN-140 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Neither; each needs `discard`. Earlier clarification of 2026-11-02: `free` no longer exists (OD59); a deferred call's Copy result is dropped with `discard`, while a `Result` or `Option` cannot be discarded (OD62) and is handled by a named function that returns `Unit` ([decisions](decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-authority)). Revised 2026-11-02: `defer` takes a call whose result is `Unit` ([decisions](decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-audit)) **Canonical current deferred-result rule: the Unit-only audit rule below; the earlier discard-inside-defer fragment is superseded, and authority rule 9 refers to the Unit-only rule.** |
1271:| OD156 | <a id="od156"></a>The capability of `get_args` (added 2026-10-31, raised while reviewing the builtins catalog of spec readability 2). Question: Which capability the builtin `get_args` requires, or whether the builtin is removed. Bears on TRUST-4, STMT-7, TRUST-9 and OPEN-136. The current compiler: Check gives `get_args()` the result `HeapArray<String>` and requires `Process` (E0520 with `Env` only); the builtin has no implementation on either consumer (bug 347). STMT-7's rationale says command-line arguments come from `std.args` with `Env`, and OD8 gives `main` the root resource value (TRUST-9). Options: (a) `Env`, as `std.args` requires; (b) `Process`, as the current compiler's signature says; (c) another capability, or none; (d) remove the builtin, and command-line arguments come from `std.args`. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised while reviewing the builtins catalog of spec readability 2, 2026-10-31). | see OPEN-156 | see OPEN-156 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Superseded by the authority decision of 2026-11-01: `get_args` requires `with(Env)`, and `with(Alloc)` when it allocates. Revised 2026-11-02 by the owner's rule: it also takes the `Environment` grant, and an allocator when it allocates ([decisions](decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-filesystem)) |
