394:The assertion production is a common-prefix parser proposal: after parsing an initial parenthesized contract expression, it accepts optional `by path(args)`; after a nonparenthesized contract expression it rejects that suffix. This restriction reflects the approved `assert(e) by thm(...)` spelling; it is not an approved extension to `assert e by ...`.
396:`deferred_call` is a parsed postfix call whose final suffix is `call_args` (a method call counts); its result must be Unit. It shares the section-5 postfix parser and rejects a binary expression, constructor or block. That syntactic restriction is the OPEN-19 recommendation. `statement_block` means a braced statement-mode block under the OPEN-16 algorithm; `arm_block` uses the surrounding match mode; `arm_expr` means section-5 expr without a block-root alternative. `value_contents` is precisely the sequence/tail selection described in that algorithm, whose EBNF expansion remains OPEN-16. These are explicit parameterized parsing obligations, not unexplained extra terminals.
414:cast          = unary, {"as", cast_type, {postfix_suffix}} ;
419:postfix       = primary, {postfix_suffix} ;
420:postfix_suffix = call_args | "[", expr, "]" | ".", IDENT, [method_tail] ;
446:`head_expr` is the same precedence hierarchy with a separate **head primary**: at enclosing `(`/`[` depth zero it has no constructor-body suffix. Entering grouping, call parentheses, array brackets or index brackets restores ordinary expr for the contents. Thus `if LIMIT {…}` ends the head, while `if test(P { x: 1 }) {…}` permits a literal inside parentheses. `as` also terminates the source of a range borrow, so that source must use the place grammar below rather than unrestricted expr. [OD16](../docs/decisions.md#od16), [OD34](../docs/decisions.md#od34) fix the head rule, not a casing convention.
463:| constructors `S { f: e }`, `E::V { f: e }`, puns | enum separator [OD77](../docs/decisions.md#od77), argument-based enum inference, otherwise explicit [OD129](../docs/decisions.md#od129); struct literal shell slice 1; puns OPEN-20; empty struct literal already admitted by OD65 and approved slice 1 |
477:`requires` sees parameters at entry and permitted declarations; `ensures` sees result and surviving parameters after cleanup, never body locals or an implicit entry snapshot; loop clauses/assertions see their lexical scope including eligible ghost locals. The November 2 [audit](../docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-audit) refines [OD143](../docs/decisions.md#od143). No `old`, quantifier, implication, equivalence or snapshot source form ([OD117](../docs/decisions.md#od117)); such properties are Lean theorems. `#[proof(T)]` means a typed theorem path T, not a type argument or string.
520:Raw types are `*const T` / `*mut T` (slice 24). Dereference/store use unary `*` and place assignment; `as` is not a blanket pointer/integer conversion mechanism: reference-to-raw conversion is gated by [OD98](../docs/decisions.md#od98). Reference-to-reference casts are refused ([OD103](../docs/decisions.md#od103)); pointer/integer round trips use Q3.9’s `expose` / `from_exposed`, not an invented `as` conversion. Integers carry no pointer provenance; every raw access requires typed validity/provenance/aliasing premises (Core Q3.7–Q3.11). Reference-derived pointers stay confined to the borrow and can never reach a kept parameter; kept retention transfers an owned allocation H by value and returns a linear Kept<H> receipt ([OD64](../docs/decisions.md#od64), Core Q3.12). A write derived from shared &T remains forbidden ([OD112](../docs/decisions.md#od112)).
522:Slice 25 declares C bindings as `extern fn`, optionally `trusted extern fn`, explicit `with(...)`, mandatory semicolon and no body. `#[link_name = "symbol"]` and `#[export_name = "symbol"]` payload forms are recommendations (OPEN-15), not an inferred `extern "C" {…}` block. Direct calls only: foreign function values and `extern "C" fn` types are not admitted ([OD4](../docs/decisions.md#od4)). FFI-safe types exclude enums ([OD110](../docs/decisions.md#od110)); C structs cross by value at every size ([OD122](../docs/decisions.md#od122)). `kept buf: H`, no marker meaning call-only, and the paired receipt discipline are already decided by the [full Q3.12 row](../docs/archive/decisions-2026-11-05/core_rows.json). The submit contract names a paired extern taking exactly Kept<H> and returning H; borrow-derived pointers are forbidden there. A kept raw pointer is not a substitute for the owned allocation. OPEN-23 concerns remaining raw API qualification and transcription, not these settled choices. Semantic validity obligations must not be replaced by inventing an `unsafe {…}` source block.
542:This is a manual conflict inventory, not the output of a generator. A mechanical FIRST/FOLLOW test must be run on the owner-approved expansions and contextual-token model before encoding the independent grammar in Lean. The frozen [conflict ledger](../legacy/grammar/ll1_known_conflicts.tsv) and [EBNF](../legacy/grammar/concrete.ebnf) are useful controls, not proof that this revised grammar is LL(1).
